{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"plan-pentest-remediation","kind":"prompt","title":"Plan remediation after a penetration test","description":"Turns a received penetration test report into a remediation plan with validated findings, re-rated risk, owners, deadlines, compensating controls, disputes and a retest scope.","category":"security-operations","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["plan","maintain"],"role":["security-engineer","engineering-manager","tech-lead"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["document","text"],"output":["plan","table","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","invocation":"user","effort":"standard","interaction":"one-shot","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"intermediate","depth":"specific","tags":["penetration-testing","remediation-plan","risk-rating","retest","findings-tracking"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["prepare-penetration-test","prioritize-vulnerability-backlog","write-bug-bounty-report"]},"args":[{"name":"report","description":"The findings from the report - title, severity as rated by the tester, affected assets, description, evidence summary and recommendation for each. Remove secrets and credentials.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"context","description":"Asset criticality and exposure, team ownership, release cadence, any compliance or customer deadline driving the test, and fix deadlines your policy sets.","type":"text"}],"output_contract":{"format":"markdown","sections":["Summary","Findings review","Remediation plan","Disputes and risk acceptance","Retest scope","Report back"]},"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"<context>\nA penetration test report is the start of the work, and many reports end up as a PDF nobody acts on until the next test finds the same issues. Severity in the report is the tester's view, often a CVSS base score without your context: an internet-facing admin panel with a medium finding can matter more than a high finding on an isolated test host, and findings that chain together (information disclosure plus weak authorisation) can be worse than any one alone. Some findings are false positives or out of date, and should be disputed with evidence rather than silently ignored. Fixes need an owner who can make the change, a deadline that matches the risk, and a check that the fix addresses the root cause, not just the tested example: one reflected XSS usually means the output encoding approach is wrong across the app. Retests are usually cheaper when scoped to the fixed findings and booked before the fixes start.\n</context>\n\n<task>\nBuild a remediation plan from:\n\n<report>\n{{report}}\n</report>\n{{#context}}\n\n<context_notes>\n{{context}}\n</context_notes>\n{{/context}}\n\n1. Summary: number of findings by tester severity, the themes behind them (for example access control, input handling, configuration, patching), and the two or three issues that matter most for this organisation.\n2. Findings review: for each finding, confirm it is reproducible from the evidence (or list what to check), re-rate it with your context (exposure, data sensitivity, exploit difficulty, chaining with other findings) and say why if the rating changes.\n3. Remediation plan: for each finding, the root-cause fix (not just the tested instance), a short-term mitigation if the fix takes time, the owner team, a deadline based on the re-rated severity and the policy in the context, and how to verify. Group findings that share a root cause into one work item.\n4. Disputes and risk acceptance: findings to dispute with the tester (with the evidence needed), and findings to accept with a compensating control, an approver at the right level and an expiry date.\n5. Retest scope: which findings to retest, what evidence to give the tester, and when to book it.\n6. Report back: a short status format for leadership or the customer who asked for the test.\n</task>\n\n<constraints>\n- Do not downgrade a finding without a stated reason tied to the context.\n- Never invent CVE numbers, exploitability claims or tester statements not in the report.\n- Deadlines in the context policy take precedence; if none is given, propose defaults and label them as proposals.\n- Do not include credentials, session tokens or exploit payloads from the report in the plan.\n- Separate what you verified from what you inferred. Mark inferences as such.\n- When you do not know, say \"I don't know\" once and state what would settle it.\n</constraints>\n\n<output_format>\n## Summary\nShort paragraph and a count table.\n\n## Findings review\nTable: ID | Title | Tester severity | Re-rated | Why | Reproduced?\n\n## Remediation plan\nTable: Work item | Findings | Root-cause fix | Mitigation now | Owner | Deadline | Verify by.\n\n## Disputes and risk acceptance\nTwo short lists.\n\n## Retest scope\nBullets.\n\n## Report back\nA fenced block with a status template.\n</output_format>","steps":[]}
